          rubric: ["Uses Roy's method: per-capita spending × trade-area population, or an equivalent", "Labels every assumption and how you'd verify it", "Computes share of market = sales ÷ MPo and the 33%/40% lines", "Draws the large- vs. small-market conclusion (IQ and PEF vs. share of voice)"],
          model: "**Roofing, greater Sacramento (assumptions):** about 300,000 owner-occupied homes in the service area, a roof roughly every 25 years (4% a year) = 12,000 roofs a year × about $15,000 average = **MPo ≈ $180M**.\n\n**Prospect:** $4M a year → share of market ≈ **2.2%**.\n\n**Lines:** 33% ≈ $59M, 40% ≈ $72M. The ceiling isn't the constraint for years.\n\n**Lever:** this is a large market, so ad dollars buy only a thin share of voice against dozens of roofers. The win has to come from a high impact quotient (the owner's real story, a specific standard) and a high PEF (reviews, photo documentation and fast quotes), exactly the large-market pressure Roy describes."
