      title: "A Coiled Cobra Called \"Statistics\": read the trend, distrust averages and assume the worst slot",
      minutes: 8,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "Magical Worlds of the Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 2001)", section: "Chapter 39 · A Coiled Cobra Called “Statistics” · pp. 94–96", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Figures lie when liars figure. You’ve got to be very careful with numbers.", page: "94" },
        { text: "you should never assume your ad is going to run at any time other than the worst time allowable by contract.", page: "96" }
      ],
      idea: `Roy's view of numbers: figures lie when liars figure. He names two opposite dangers.

**Too narrow.** A coin lands heads 50% of the time, which any knothead with a quarter can "disprove": two flips, two heads; ten flips, seven heads. He only proves he doesn't understand that **statistical properties emerge only on large scales.**

**Too general.** Then there's the statistician who drowned wading across a river with an *average* depth of four feet.

**The ratings-book lesson.** A new research employee asked whether to use only the latest ratings period or a four-book average. Roy's answer was "that depends," and he showed why on a whiteboard:
1. **6, 8, 10, 12** share (spring to winter): the four-book average is 9, but the numbers are climbing steadily, so the latest 12 is probably closer to the truth. Let the rep pitch the latest book.
2. **12, 10, 8, 6:** the same average of 9, but this rep is the slippery one who'll pitch the four-book average. The truth is 6.
3. **11, 12, 12, 7:** a sudden drop. Sampling error is real, so if you investigate and find no reason for the drop, let the rep use the four-book average.

The rule for media buyers is "Look for the Truth"; for reps, "Help them find it." A sidebar fable agrees: the devil isn't worried when a man picks up a piece of Truth, because he plans to help him organize it. (Chapter 23's "93% nonverbal" myth is the same disease: organized truth, misapplied.)

**Hot Tip: the broad-rotator lie.** A station averages 100,000 listeners from 6–9 a.m., 80,000 from 9 a.m.–3 p.m., 90,000 from 3–7 p.m. and 30,000 from 7 p.m.–midnight. Ask the computer to analyze 18 ads a week from 6 a.m. to midnight and it assumes even rotation, one ad per hour, so it reports **71,667 people per airing**. But the scheduler can legally put most of your ads from 7 p.m. to midnight (the strong hours belong to advertisers whose contracts specify 6 a.m.–7 p.m.), so you'll actually reach **30,000**.

His rules:
- TV audiences are far bigger at night; radio's are bigger in the daytime. **Never agree to a scheduling window that crosses the 7 p.m. hour.** 6 a.m.–7 p.m. is fine and 7 p.m.–midnight is fine, but 3–10 p.m. is not.
- Prime time depends on the late news. With news at 10 p.m. (typical in mid-America), radio prime is roughly 6 a.m.–7 p.m. and TV prime 7–11 p.m. With news at 11 p.m. (typical on both coasts), radio prime is roughly 6 a.m.–8 p.m. and TV prime 8 p.m.–midnight.
- Off-prime schedules are fine, but **never assume your ad runs at any time other than the worst one the contract allows, and never buy a broad rotator, however cheap.**

(A rep who gave you this book is probably an honest one.)`,
