      title: "How to Facilitate Brainstorming: send the note 24 hours ahead so introverts can think first",
      minutes: 5,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "Magical Worlds of the Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 2001)", section: "Chapter 40 · How to Facilitate Brainstorming · p. 97", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "When you understand preferences, everybody wins.", page: "97" }
      ],
      idea: `A one-page chapter with one tool you can use this week.

**The problem.** Extraverts invented brainstorming. They're stimulated by things outside themselves and "talk to think," working ideas out by saying them. But more than half the population is introverted, and introverts, equally brilliant, "think to talk." They rarely tell you what they're *thinking*, only what they've *already thought about*. So in a typical brainstorm they sit quietly, gathering information to process later when they're alone. The meeting ends before their best ideas exist. Roy's verdict: what a waste.

**The fix.** Send everyone a detailed note **24 hours ahead** that explains the issues to be discussed in tomorrow's session. Watch how each type reads it:
- **Extraverts** see the note as an invitation and probably won't think about it again until the meeting.
- **Introverts** treat the note as a work assignment and start organizing their thoughts right away.

Twenty-four hours later the introverts arrive having processed and organized their thinking, and they'll amaze you with the quality of what they say. Then the extraverts, sparked by the discussion the introverts open with, perform better than they usually do. Both preferences get used instead of one.

The cross-reference is Chapter 26, "Getting to Know Yourself," on preference profiling. Introversion and extraversion are *preferences*, not abilities, and the point of knowing them is to "do unto others the way they prefer to be done unto." This chapter is that principle as a meeting habit.

The sidebar quote from neurologist Richard Cytowic deepens it. Not everything we know or can do is accessible to language; some of our knowledge is off-limits even to our own inner thoughts, which may be why we're so often at odds with ourselves. There's more going on in our minds than we consciously know. Giving people time to reflect is how you give that hidden knowledge a chance to surface in words.

**What a good 24-hour note contains** (our checklist, built from Roy's "detailed note that explains the issues"): the problem in one sentence, the background and any numbers, the specific questions to be answered, the constraints, and what you need to walk out with.`,
