      id: "mw-041",
      part: "p2",
      chapter: 41,
      chapterTitle: "What We Say vs. What We Do",
      pages: "98–99",
      title: "What We Say vs. What We Do: New Coke and the trap of left-brain surveys",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "Magical Worlds of the Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 2001)", section: "Chapter 41 · What We Say vs. What We Do · pp. 98–99", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "a beverage is more than its flavor; a beverage is its marketing.", page: "98" },
        { text: "New Coke is the kind of answer you get when you think with only half your brain.", page: "99" }
      ],
      idea: `Enormous time, skill and money went into consumer research before New Coke launched in 1985, and none of it revealed America's emotional attachment to the original.

**The first clue was in the questions.** Asked "What's your favorite drink?", most people said Coke. Asked "What do you have in your pantry right now?", the answers changed: some Coke, some Pepsi, others whatever was on sale, often a generic cola. What people *say* and what they *do* had already split.

**The left-brain logic.** In 1983, Roy Stout, Coca-Cola USA's head of market research, asked why Coke was losing share when it had twice as many vending machines, dominated fountain sales, had more shelf space, outspent Pepsi on advertising and was competitively priced. Look at the Pepsi Challenge, he said, and you have to start asking about taste. Brian Dyson, Coca-Cola USA's president, agreed that maybe tastes had changed and Coke's distinctive "bite" now read as harsh. Extensive research followed, and a new, improved formula was ordered.

**The result.** Long before anyone bought a can, America had decided it hated New Coke, even though it was the flavor people had chosen in tens of thousands of taste tests. Surveyors asked which cola we'd buy, and we overwhelmingly picked the flavor that became New Coke. But a beverage is more than its flavor. A beverage is its marketing, and Coca-Cola had spent 99 years convincing us that Coke was "the real thing," woven into American life. The taste test measured the tongue. The purchase came from the associations.

**The principle.** When surveyed, most people honestly tell you what they *believe* they'd do. That's rarely what they actually do when the choice is in front of them.

Roy dismisses the popular theory that New Coke was a clever ploy to remind us how much we loved the original. The facts say otherwise: a left-brain survey was run and a left-brain decision was made from it (the left/right split is Chapter 6). He ends with Donald Keough, Coca-Cola's president when Classic Coke returned: some cynics say we planned it all, but the truth is we're not that smart.

This connects forward to Chapter 42, where the wording of a question decides its answer, and to Chapter 43, where a brand turns out to be the associations attached to a name rather than the product's specs.`,
