      id: "mw-043",
      part: "p2",
      chapter: 43,
      chapterTitle: "BrandingBrandingBranding",
      pages: "102–103",
      title: "BrandingBrandingBranding: it's the message, not the targeting",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "Magical Worlds of the Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 2001)", section: "Chapter 43 · BrandingBrandingBranding · pp. 102–103", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "A brand is the sum total of all the mental associations, good and bad, that are triggered by a name.", page: "102" },
        { text: "Branding is about the message.", page: "103" }
      ],
      idea: `People talk about "branding" as if it were mysterious and complex. Roy peels off the hype. **If advertising is "getting your name out," branding is "attaching something to your name."** A brand is the sum of all the mental associations, good and bad, that your name triggers. So the real question is what your name stands for in the public's mind, and what pops into someone's head when they hear it.

He makes the point in a short Q&A. "You mean it actually matters what I say in my ads?" Yes, it actually matters. "But I was told all I had to do was reach the right people with my message." And you believed it?

**Targeting is overrated.** Demographic and psychographic targeting have a place in planning, but their importance has long been overstated. Roy's evidence is the dotcom crash. The NASDAQ fell in 2000 because internet companies reported revenues far below projections while burning investor cash faster. Most of their business plans depended on selling advertising, and the internet's pitch to advertisers was precision targeting: ads matched to audience profiles, reaching the right user at the right time. In November 1999, USA Today reported that ad networks were secretly gathering data through cookies and that marketing databases held more information about buying habits than ever. Eight months later, in July 2000, the Potomac Tech Journal reported that more than 9 in 10 web businesses were unhappy with the results of their banner ads. Advertising.com's response was to promise to target better. Roy's reaction: "Huh?"

**The principle:** the message itself matters far more than the vehicle that delivers it. Successful branding depends on speaking to the customer **in the language of the customer about what matters to the customer.** The goal is to be the name customers think of immediately whenever they, *or anyone they know*, need what you sell. The analytical internet reps preached that psychographic targeting was all that mattered. Their ads failed, and most of those reps were soon out of work.

His closing check: you haven't built your branding campaign around reaching the right people and forgotten about associative ad copy, have you?

This builds on Chapter 16 (branding happens in long-term, associative memory) and Chapter 35 (share of mind is earned by the message's impact, not just bought with exposure). It also explains the Heroin sidebar in Chapter 29: the name Bayer attached did the selling.

A 2020s footnote that's ours, not Roy's: on Meta, broad targeting and automated audiences have made his argument even stronger. When the algorithm finds the audience, **the creative is the targeting**. The message decides who stops and who buys.`,
