      id: "mw-044",
      part: "p2",
      chapter: 44,
      chapterTitle: "Frosting",
      pages: "104–105",
      title: "Frosting: replace predictable phrases with unexpected ones",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "Magical Worlds of the Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 2001)", section: "Chapter 44 · Frosting · pp. 104–105", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "The essence of Frosting is to replace common, predictable phrases with unexpected, interesting ones.", page: "104" },
        { text: "The goal is simply to surprise Broca with elegant combinations of words.", page: "104" }
      ],
      idea: `This is the first of the writing tools Roy borrowed from artists through business problem topology (Chapter 28). It's named after the poet Robert Frost, and Roy calls it the simplest and gentlest technique for turning dull communication into razor-edged wordsmanship.

**The essence:** replace common, predictable phrases with unexpected, interesting ones. **The goal:** surprise Broca. That's the "tollbooth" from Chapter 19, the part of the brain that screens incoming information, turns away anything it "sees coming," and lets only the new and surprising onto the road to the imagination. Predictable phrases get waved off. Unexpected combinations pay the toll.

**The demonstration** (read both versions on pp. 104–105; we won't reproduce them). Roy "de-Frosts" Frost's poem "Misgiving," keeping its story and structure but rewriting every line in flat, everyday phrasing: the leaves "shouted," "got sleepy," "tried to convince" the wind to stay, and the stanza even ends on a shrug of "go figure." Then he prints Frost's original. The story is the same in both. The leaves cry out that they'll follow the wind wherever it goes, but sleepiness overtakes them on the way; they end up asking the wind to stay with them, and answer its later calls with ever fainter stirrings that carry them nowhere. In the last stanza, the poet hopes that when he's free to go looking for the knowledge beyond the bounds of life, he won't find that he'd rather rest.

The de-Frosted version, Roy says, reads like a tacky third-grade limerick. In the original, notice two things:
1. **Unusual combinations of common words.** Frost rarely uses a fancy word. The magic is in pairing ordinary words in ways you've never heard (a "summoning blast," a "reluctant whirl"), which creates vivid, compact mental images.
2. **An early verb.** He drops you into the action right away. The poem opens mid-cry, in motion, with no setup.

**What Frosting is not:** it isn't rewriting the message, adding claims or reaching for a thesaurus. The structure and meaning stay put; only the predictable phrasing changes. That's why Roy calls it gentle, and why it's the safest place to start before the more radical tools that follow: Seussing (Ch 45), Being Monet (Ch 46), which he calls "radical, accelerated Frosting," and Robert Frank (Ch 51).

**His exercise** (margin, p. 105): find a few paragraphs you've written and, *without changing the message structurally*, replace every common, predictable phrase with an unexpected, interesting one. Whip a little Frosting on it.`,
