        body: "**The joy is in the seeking.** A Reel that hands over a complete answer (\"Here's how to use the clutch\") ends the seeking. One that opens a question bigger than the clip (\"Our coaches can tell in the first 30 seconds whether you'll ride by Sunday. Here's what they're watching for…\") invites the viewer to keep stretching, and to book to find out.\n\n**A working reading of the glossary terms** (Roy doesn't spell out the method in this book): think of a campaign as a few recurring elements, such as the same coach's voice and the same \"Saturday scared / Sunday riding\" arc, plus a small third element that changes each time (a new student, a new fear, a new line). That changing element is the \"horseshoe nail.\" Left alone, the audience compresses the recurring elements into a predictable blur. Swapping the third element (transition) keeps the pattern alive, and the moment everything clicks into place is the convergence, the hook. Treat this as a hypothesis to test, not as Roy's rulebook."
